    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d735">

                <pb facs="0634" n="622" sameAs="frus:frus1942v04/pg622"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">890E.01/152: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Beirut</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Gwynn</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Beirut</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-08-25T14:00:00+02:00">August 25, 1942—2
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received August 25—12:12 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>309. Department’s 134, August 19, 9 p.m., and my 281, August 12, 1 p.m. I have
                    now received a copy of Casey’s letter to Catroux concerning elections which will
                    go by pouch. The sentence concerning the United States reads as follows: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“As you are aware the recognition of the independence of both states by
                            Egypt and Iraq is likely to depend on elections being held <pb
                                facs="0635" n="623" xml:id="pg623"/> and there is reason to believe
                            that the United States Government take a similar view.”</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gwynn</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
